    "result_text": "Build succeeds and the test passes. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe membership tier was a string (`GOLD`/`SILVER`/`BRONZE`) derived by `MembershipTierEvaluator` and exposed as `membershipTier` on the owner response. I replaced it with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`MembershipLevelEvaluator`** (renamed from `MembershipTierEvaluator`) \u2014 now computes the level per the spec: start at 1, +1 for an email on file, +1 when `namesakeCount` is 0, capped at 3 (with `MAX_LEVEL = 3` and a comment noting level 4 is reserved for tenure). Since the old GOLD-household branch is gone, the class no longer depends on `ClinicService`, so I dropped that injection \u2014 it keeps the class to its single responsibility.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 maps `membershipLevel` via `membershipLevelEvaluator.levelFor(owner)` instead of the old `membershipTier` mapping.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 replaced the `membershipTier` (string) property in the `Owner` schema with `membershipLevel` (integer, 1\u20134), which is what regenerates `OwnerDto` at build time. This removes `membershipTier` from the response entirely.\n\n**On the audit line:** the spec says \"anything that recorded the tier (such as the create audit line) must record the level instead.\" I checked `OwnerAuditLogger` \u2014 its create line records `id`, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate` only; it never recorded the tier. So there was nothing there to switch to the level, and I left it unchanged rather than adding scope.\n\nThe new acceptance test (`coreNumericLevel`: unique name + email \u2192 level `1 + email + namesake0 = 3`) passes, and `BUILD SUCCESS` confirms the generated DTO and the full suite are green.",
